<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  CI ONLY build overrides for the driver build VERIFICATION job.

  This file is imported ONLY in CI, via the documented MSBuild property
  /p:ForceImportAfterCppTargets=<path to this file> (which Microsoft.Cpp.targets
  imports at the very end, after the WDK's own targets). The real VM and
  production driver build does NOT pass that property, so it is completely
  unaffected: it still stamps, verifies, packages, and signs the INF and the
  .sys exactly as before.

  What this job verifies:

  The CI "Drivers (WDK build)" job exists only to prove that the driver C and
  C++ compiles and links clean as a real kernel mode binary (toolset
  WindowsKernelModeDriver10.0, /kernel, KMDF) and passes the Universal driver
  ApiValidator. INF stamping, INF verification, catalog generation, signing,
  loading, and runtime behaviour are verified in the disposable VM (see
  vm/VERIFICATION-RUNBOOK.md), never on a host and never in this build job.

  What is neutralised, and why:

  * InfVerif: the GitHub hosted windows 2022 runner ships a WDK (10.0.26100)
    whose InfVerif task cannot load its own companion DLL ("Unable to load DLL
    'x86\InfVerif.dll'", HRESULT 0x8007007E). INF verification is a packaging
    check, not part of compiling and linking, so it is replaced with a no op.

  * DriverPackageTarget: packaging the driver (copying the stamped INF into a
    driver package, then catalog generation) is a deployment step, not part of
    compiling and linking. It is replaced with a no op so the only driver with
    an INF (fsfilter) is verified the same way as the others: compile, link,
    ApiValidator. (It also sidesteps the fact that no-opping InfVerif above
    removes the StampInf step that would otherwise produce the packaged INF.)

  Driver signing is disabled separately and cleanly with /p:SignMode=Off on the
  msbuild command line (CI has no code signing certificate; test signing
  happens in the VM).

  MSBuild honours the LAST definition of a target by name, and because this
  file is imported after the WDK targets, these empty targets win.
-->
<Project xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <Target Name="InfVerif" />
  <Target Name="DriverPackageTarget" />
</Project>
